This is a solid attempt at a more advanced Spring task! You've tackled all four requirements and shown you understand the concepts, but there are some execution issues that hold it back from being excellent.
What you handled well:
All requirements covered - You've converted to explicit bean definitions, created multiple variations, extracted to separate projects, and attempted customization. The multi-module Maven setup shows you understand project separation.
Good understanding of @Qualifier usage - Your constructor injection with @Qualifier("webRecommender") demonstrates you get how to distinguish between beans.
Clean separation - Having the recommender as a separate module and importing it as a dependency is exactly what was asked for.
Where things got messy:
Inconsistent bean management approach - You're mixing explicit @Bean definitions in AppConfig with implicit @Component annotations. Pick one approach and stick with it. If you're going explicit, remove the annotations.
Your component scanning is off - You're scanning for "implementation" package but your beans are defined explicitly in config. This creates confusion about which beans are actually being used.
The customization step isn't clear - Step 4 asks you to customize the extracted bean, but I can't see where you've actually changed the behavior meaningfully. The comment about "overriding" doesn't show actual customization.
Package structure inconsistency - Some files have implementation package, others don't. This makes it hard to follow which beans are coming from where.
Quick fixes:
Either go full explicit or full implicit - If using @Bean methods, remove @Component annotations entirely. Or vice versa.
Make the customization obvious - Change the WebRecommender to return different courses or add new functionality to clearly show it's been customized.
Clean up the component scanning - Make sure your @ComponentScan actually matches where your annotated classes are.
The core concepts are there, you just need to clean up the execution. This feels like you understood each step individually but didn't quite tie them together into a cohesive whole.
